# What an AI assistant should not spend its context on (CI-CD-AND-TOOLS-v8 § 2.2).
#
# The rule of thumb from the canon: hide what is large and opaque, keep what is small
# and descriptive. Every config in the root stays visible on purpose — package.json,
# svelte.config.js, vite.config.ts, tsconfig.json, eslint.config.js are exactly the
# files an assistant reads to learn which adapter, which CSP, which base path and which
# gates this project has. Hiding them saves a few kilobytes and buys a suggestion to
# "add eslint" to a project that has had it for months.

# Dependencies and build output
node_modules/
build/
dist/
.svelte-kit/

# Lockfiles: enormous, and nothing in them answers a question about this project
*.lock
package-lock.json

# Test and audit artefacts, regenerated on every run
playwright-report/
test-results/
blob-report/
.lighthouseci/

# Secrets
.env
.env.*

# 9 MB of photographs. Nothing in them is readable as text.
static/images/

# Working files, EXCEPT the documentation.
#
# `.private/docs/` is the project's canon — decisions (adr/), specifications (specs/),
# analyses — and DOCUMENTATION-v8 says plainly that it is context for an assistant, not
# noise. It was excluded here along with everything else in `.private/`, which meant the
# 41 files describing why this project is the way it is were invisible to the assistant
# reading it.
#
# The two lines have to be in this order and this shape: gitignore syntax cannot
# re-include a file whose parent DIRECTORY is excluded, so the exclusion has to name the
# children (`.private/*`) rather than the folder.
.private/*
!.private/docs/

# A read-only copy of a neighbouring project, kept for reference. Not ours to change.
.read_for_AI/

.git/
